# Performance rules

The hot path is the frame path (`docs/design.ja.md` §2.10, §11.5): input dispatch, `Batch` flush, invalidation, materialization, layout, DisplayList and HitTestList build, upload, present. It also covers pointer move and hover handling. Construction of nodes, styles, and resources may allocate; the steady-state frame does not.

These rules come from the shape of Cysharp's R3, ZLinq, ZString, MemoryPack, and MagicOnion (read in `.repos/`), applied to this framework. A claim that a rule was followed is a measurement, not a reading of the code (`.cursor/rules/bench.mdc`).

## Allocation

- The frame path does not allocate in steady state. The Zero-Alloc tests (design §11.3) assert it; an allowed exception needs an Issue and a design entry.
- No LINQ, `params` arrays, `string` concatenation or interpolation into `string`, boxing (`object` parameters, non-generic interfaces on structs, `Enum.HasFlag` on old paths, `foreach` over an interface), or lambdas that capture on the frame path. A `static` lambda is fine: its delegate is cached.
- `foreach` over a `List<T>` field goes through `CollectionsMarshal.AsSpan`; over a `Dictionary` it uses the struct enumerator directly, never `IEnumerable<T>`.
- A cache or pool that grows on demand has a hard upper bound and a counter of the requests it refused at the bound. Its steady-state limit is not enough: in S8b the glyph atlas added pages while every plot was in use in the frame, and a caller that never started a new frame would have added pages without end (`GlyphAtlas.PageLimit`, `RefusedCount`).
- Buffers that live across frames are owned and reused: reset the used length, grow only when full (`DisplayListBuffer`, design §5.0.1). Short-lived scratch comes from `ArrayPool<T>.Shared` or `stackalloc` (fixed small size only), and is returned in a `finally`. Scratch for runs that nest (a flex container's run and its items' runs) comes from a per-thread `ScratchStack<T>`, given back in a `finally`: with `ArrayPool<T>.Shared`, which keeps one array of a size on a thread, a nested container's array goes to the pool's shared stacks, and a `Return` in the steady state allocated now and then on the runners (4,048 bytes in one call, Issue #92). Clear a returned array only when `RuntimeHelpers.IsReferenceOrContainsReferences<T>()`.
- A `[ThreadStatic]` scratch buffer may back a builder that is disposed before the method returns (ZString, MagicOnion). In debug builds the rent takes the buffer out of the slot, so nested use throws instead of corrupting it.
- Text that changes every frame uses `Text(TextBuilder)` and writes into the node's own buffer (design §2.13), not `Text(Func<string>)`. Whether a text update joins the Zero-Alloc scenarios is decided after S8 measures shaping.
- An `async` API on a repeated path returns `ValueTask` backed by a pooled `IValueTaskSource` (`ManualResetValueTaskSourceCore<T>`), not a new `Task` each time.

## Collections of subscribers and handles

- A subscriber list that changes during notification is an intrusive linked list whose node is the subscription itself (R3 `Subject`): no separate `IDisposable` per subscription, O(1) removal, and a version stamp so nodes added during a notification are skipped without copying the list.
- An add/remove set keyed by slot uses a free list: removal nulls the slot, the index is the removal key, and the next add reuses the first empty slot (R3 `FreeListCore`). Do not use `ImmutableArray` or copy-on-write arrays for lists that change at run time.
- A fixed small number of items is held in fields, not an array (R3 `Disposable.Combine` up to eight).

## Dispatch and generics

- No interface or virtual dispatch per element on the frame path. Dispatch once per call (a `switch` on a type tag, a sealed class hierarchy matched once), or use a generic method constrained to a struct (`where T : struct, IFoo`) so the JIT and NativeAOT specialize it.
- Per-type data is a `static class Cache<T>` field, filled by generated code or a static constructor without reflection, never a `Dictionary<Type, ...>` lookup on the frame path. JIT-constant checks (`typeof(T) == typeof(int)`, `RuntimeHelpers.IsReferenceOrContainsReferences<T>()`) choose fast paths at no cost.
- A struct walker over a tree (`ITraverser`-style, ZLinq) iterates the arena without allocating; its explicit stack comes from a pool and is returned on `Dispose`.
- Iteration over a sequence the framework owns uses a single `TryGetNext(out T)` call per element rather than `MoveNext` + `Current` (ZLinq).

## Fast path and slow path

- Throwing lives in a separate method marked `[DoesNotReturn]` (and `[StackTraceHidden]` when the frame is noise), so the caller stays small enough to inline. Throw helpers in this repository are `ThrowHelper.*`.
- Rare work (growing a buffer, requesting a new segment) lives in a `[MethodImpl(MethodImplOptions.NoInlining)]` method; the common check stays inline (MemoryPack `GetSpanReference` / `RequestNewBuffer`).
- `[MethodImpl(MethodImplOptions.AggressiveInlining)]` only where a measurement shows an effect, and the PR says which.
- A writer over a buffer may be a `ref struct` holding a `ref` field and a remaining length instead of a `Span` field (MemoryPack `MemoryPackWriter`). Bounds are checked once per reservation, not per byte.

## Memory layout

- Data read together is stored together. The arena stores `UiNode` structs and is walked by index; GPU-bound data is SoA and copied with `MemoryMarshal.AsBytes` (design §5.0.1).
- Unmanaged structs that cross to native code or the GPU have `[StructLayout(LayoutKind.Sequential)]`; others may use `LayoutKind.Auto`.
- A field added to a struct on the frame path goes where the struct has padding, with `Unsafe.SizeOf` read before and after. In L5 a 4-byte field before the measurement cache's entries made it 464 bytes a node instead of 456 and the first layout of 10,101 nodes 7 % slower; after the flags it fit the padding (PR #112).
- A loop over children on the frame path reads only what it needs, and work for a part of the tree the change did not touch stays constant. In L2 a block copied each child's style before checking that the child was in the flow (a change in the absolute scene 21.6 µs, 10.4 µs after), and every relayout walked a subtree that was already hidden (a sibling's change with 10,100 hidden nodes 145 µs, 1.16 µs after; the review found it) (PR #125).
- A check per child for a feature that most children do not use reads a flag on the node, set when the style is set, and comes after the test that most children take. In L3, reading each absolutely positioned child's four minimums and maximums made a change in the absolute scene 5.2 % slower than main, and a flag (`LayoutState.HasKeywordLimits`) 0.7 %; checking a flex item's keyword limits before its base-size memo made one change with CSS's defaults 3.1 % slower, and after the memo 1.1 % (`layout-loop`, main and the branch alternated 12 times each, PR #134).
- A `ref` into an arena is never held across an operation that can grow the arena (design §2.5, Invariant 12).
- A comparison on the frame path does not use the generated `==` of a record struct: it goes through `EqualityComparer<T>` field by field and was not inlined, 19 % of the samples of a change in the absolute scene in L7. Compare the fields directly (2.22 to 1.98 µs, PR #138).
- Explicit SIMD (`Vector128<T>`, `Vector<T>`) only where a measurement shows the path is the bottleneck, with a scalar path that gives the same result, and a test that compares them.

## Claims

A speed or memory improvement is claimed with numbers against a named baseline (`.cursor/rules/bench.mdc`). Not with a hunch, an IL reading, or a micro-benchmark of a different path.

```csharp
// BAD — allocates a closure, a delegate, an enumerator, and a list every frame
var dirty = nodes.Where(n => n.Dirty.SelfDirty != DirtyFlags.None && n.Id != skip).ToList();

// GOOD — no allocation in steady state
foreach (ref readonly var node in CollectionsMarshal.AsSpan(nodes))
{
    if (node.Dirty.SelfDirty == DirtyFlags.None || node.Id == skip) continue;
    // ...
}
```
